## JWT Expiry Check Bug

The most common cause is a **units mismatch**. JWT `exp` is in **seconds**, but `Date.now()` returns **milliseconds**.

### The Bug

```javascript
// BROKEN - comparing milliseconds to seconds
const isExpired = Date.now() > payload.exp;
// Date.now() ≈ 1700000000000 (milliseconds)
// payload.exp ≈ 1700000000    (seconds)
// 1700000000000 > 1700000000 is ALWAYS true... 
// wait, that would reject everything

// More likely bug - reversed comparison or divided incorrectly
const isExpired = payload.exp > Date.now(); // reversed - always passes expired tokens
// OR
const isExpired = Date.now() / 1000 > payload.exp; // this is actually correct
```

### Common Variations of This Bug

```javascript
// Bug 1: Reversed comparison (most likely your issue)
if (payload.exp > Date.now()) {  // WRONG - backwards logic
  throw new Error('Token expired');
}

// Bug 2: Wrong units, no conversion
if (Date.now() > payload.exp) {  // WRONG - ms vs seconds, always "expired"
  throw new Error('Token expired');
}

// Bug 3: Multiplying instead of dividing
if ((Date.now() * 1000) > payload.exp) {  // WRONG - makes it even larger
  throw new Error('Token expired');
}
```

### The Fix

```javascript
const authMiddleware = (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    const token = req.headers.authorization?.split(' ')[1];

    if (!token) {
      return res.status(401).json({ error: 'No token provided' });
    }

    const payload = jwt.decode(token); // or your manual decode

    if (!payload) {
      return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Invalid token' });
    }

    // CORRECT: Convert Date.now() ms → seconds to match JWT exp
    const currentTimeInSeconds = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000);

    if (!payload.exp) {
      return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Token has no expiry' });
    }

    if (currentTimeInSeconds > payload.exp) {
      return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Token expired' });
    }

    req.user = payload;
    next();

  } catch (err) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Authentication failed' });
  }
};
```

### Better: Use jsonwebtoken's Built-in Verification

Don't manually check expiry — `jwt.verify()` handles it automatically and correctly:

```javascript
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');

const authMiddleware = (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    const token = req.headers.authorization?.split(' ')[1];

    if (!token) {
      return res.status(401).json({ error: 'No token provided' });
    }

    // verify() checks signature AND expiry automatically
    const payload = jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET);

    req.user = payload;
    next();

  } catch (err) {
    // jwt.verify throws specific errors you can handle
    if (err.name === 'TokenExpiredError') {
      return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Token expired' });
    }
    if (err.name === 'JsonWebTokenError') {
      return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Invalid token' });
    }
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Authentication failed' });
  }
};
```

### Quick Debugging Checklist

| Issue | Symptom | Fix |
|-------|---------|-----|
| Reversed `>` vs `<` | Expired tokens pass, valid tokens rejected | Flip comparison |
| Missing `/ 1000` | All tokens appear expired | Divide `Date.now()` by 1000 |
| Using `jwt.decode()` | No signature or expiry validation | Switch to `jwt.verify()` |
| No `exp` field in token | Expiry check silently skipped | Validate `exp` exists, or reject tokens without it |

**Share your actual middleware code** if the issue is different from these — the fix depends on exactly how your check is written.
